  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "At a harbour the water is $9$ m deep at high tide and $3$ m deep at low tide. The depth follows a sine wave. What is the MIDLINE depth, in metres?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "6",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "The midline is the level the wave wiggles around — right between the highest and lowest.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "The midline is the level the wave wiggles around — right between the highest and lowest."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Average them: $(9 + 3) \\div 2$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$(9 + 3) \\div 2 = 6$ metres."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "A tide graph is a wave: high, low, high, low, forever.",
    "The midline sits exactly halfway between the peak and the trough, so average them.",
    "$(9 + 3) \\div 2 = 12 \\div 2 = 6$ m. (The amplitude would be the half-GAP: $(9 - 3) \\div 2 = 3$ m.)"
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Climb up from the bottom",
      "steps": [
        "The gap from low to high is 9 − 3 = 6 m.",
        "The middle is half that gap above the low point: 3 + 3 = 6 m."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Write the equation",
      "steps": [
        "The model is depth = a·sin(x) + k, with k the midline and a the amplitude.",
        "High = k + a = 9 and low = k − a = 3. Add the two lines: 2k = 12, so k = 6."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "midline",
    "word-problem"
  ],
